KG42.register("source_edge_archive-a892a3cfb8879b8aee5126ce",{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::E::v141-c-R005-E001","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005","record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R005-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"matching.counterparties","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R005-C001","v141-c-R005-C002","v141-c-R005-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E06","v141-b-g02-E11"]},"evidence_class":"S","provenance_gate":"direct_C_edge_with_constraint_and_source_excerpt","scientific_acceptance":false,"accepted_constraint_ids":["v141-c-R005-C001","v141-c-R005-C002","v141-c-R005-C003"],"held_constraint_ids":[],"semantic_review_status":"supported","source":"COMP-166b347ab5fcd2a9","target":"COMP-486b93e7e3eb4336","source_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::V::coordination.price_signal","target_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::V::matching.counterparties","source_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","target_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::matching.counterparties","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. | Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. | Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. | Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. | Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. | Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E06","v141-b-g02-E11"]},"active":true,"source_signature":"643b658ff944acca13c474051eb56892eecba591792fb5df47b2db4461fd7f91","relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"source_science":{"mechanism_id":"v141-M01","source_definition":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges. Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period. Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","target_definition":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents. A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price. Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners. The bilateral trade power from agent n to agent m must be zero when m is outside n’s partnership set. Scope: Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n’s trading partnership set. Partnership sets specify possible trades and do not themselves enforce market participation. Scope: Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation. Partnership sets could be dynamically adapted in response to congestion; this is a proposed possibility rather than an asserted implemented procedure. Scope: Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. Condition: Congestion occurs.","constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E06"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E11"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g02-E02"]}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets \\omega_n could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma_{nm}^{\\mathrm{dist}}=\\pm\\frac{u^{\\mathrm{dist}}d_{nm}}{2}, \\forall(n,m)\\in\\Omega\\times\\omega_n, (11); where d_nm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{\\mathrm{dist}} is expressed in €/MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets ωn could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma^{dist}_{nm} = \\pm \\frac{u^{dist} d_{nm}}{2}, \\quad \\forall (n,m) \\in \\Omega \\times \\omega_n, (11) where dnm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{dist} is expressed in € /MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"opa DC power ﬂow approximation of the entire network, is bet- onter suited for meshed networks such ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"While the unique cost allocation policy does not differentiate agents, the electrical distance one might individualize too much grid tariffs. At the image of Danish public transportation system, using a zonal cost allocation policy seems a good compromise between both. In this situation the electrical network would be divided in several zones associated to distinct zonal unit fees. Each zone could be managed by a different system operator. A zone with a high price would incite outside agents not to trade with agents within, and push within agents towards self-consumption. In this sense, the zonal cost allocation policy allows to economically isolate an area. However, its efficiency strongly depends on zones’ design."}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. | Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. | Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. | Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. | Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. | Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E06","v141-b-g02-E11"]},"source_record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R005-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"matching.counterparties","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R005-C001","v141-c-R005-C002","v141-c-R005-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E06","v141-b-g02-E11"]},"paper_scope_note":"The bilateral negotiated price update and the exogenous network fee signal are separately limited in meaning; partner-set constraints, incentive intent, and ADMM computational dependencies cannot be transmitted in a mixed way."}});
